<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Trip to the Beach</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Always Pack Lunch.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Sustain conversations on a variety of topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe quantities needed for a trip using numbers (e.g., 'I need three towels and two hats.').<br>Can initiate and sustain conversations about beach trips and respond to questions (e.g., 'Where are you going this weekend?' 'We are going to the beach.' 'Do you like the beach?' 'Yes, it's fun.').<br>Can answer, in collaboration with peers, questions and discuss plans for beach trips (e.g., 'What do you bring to the beach? I bring sunscreen.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays flashcards of beach items (sunscreen, towel, swimsuit, sun hat, beach ball, cooler, flip-flops). The teacher asks, "What do we see?" "What is this?" Students respond chorally and individually. The teacher then asks, "Where do we use these things?" eliciting "At the beach!" or "For a trip to the beach!" to activate prior knowledge. Teacher holds up a pretend lunch box and asks, "What do I always pack?" leading to the theme.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the question and answer patterns: "What do you always pack for the beach?" (Teacher holds up a swimsuit flashcard) "I always pack my swimsuit." (Teacher holds up a towel flashcard) "I always pack a towel." The teacher explicitly models the sounds /b/ in 'beach' and /p/ in 'pack', exaggerating the mouth movements. The teacher also models polite requests: "Please pack your sun hat."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I pack a beach ball in my lunchbox?" (No!) "What do I pack?" (Lunch!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'beach' for eating or for swimming?" (For swimming!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Trip to the Beach".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue projected on the board or printed on a handout between two friends, Leo and Mia, planning a beach trip. Example dialogue:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <b>Mia:</b> "Hi Leo! Are you going to the beach this weekend?"<br>
                    <b>Leo:</b> "Yes, I am! I always pack my lunch and a big towel."<br>
                    <b>Mia:</b> "Great! I am going to bring my swimsuit and sunscreen. Please pack a sun hat!"<br>
                    <b>Leo:</b> "Okay, Mia! I like to build sandcastles. Do you like to collect shells?"<br>
                    <b>Mia:</b> "Yes, I do! It's fun. What else do you bring?"<br>
                    <b>Leo:</b> "I bring a beach ball and my flip-flops. We are going to have a sunny day!"
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interacting verbally to exchange information, express preferences, and make simple plans related to familiar topics, specifically a beach trip. Initiating and sustaining conversations using simple questions and affirmative responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud, then students repeat chorally. The teacher asks simple questions about the dialogue:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who is going to the beach?" (Leo and Mia.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does Leo always pack?" (His lunch and a big towel.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does Mia like to collect shells?" (Yes, she does.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does Mia ask Leo to pack?" (A sun hat.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple (affirmative and question forms), Imperatives, 'Going to'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> beach, towel, sunscreen, swimsuit, sun hat, beach ball, flip-flops, pack, bring, swim, build, collect, fun, sunny, always.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /b/ in 'beach', /p/ in 'pack'; blending /p/ /æk/ for 'pack', /s/ /ændi/ for 'sandy'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Describing trips ('I always pack lunch for the trip.'), Describing routines ('We go to the beach every Saturday.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Giving instructions politely ('Please pack a swimsuit and a pair of sandals.').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion & Choral Repetition:</b> The teacher projects incomplete sentences on the board or uses a worksheet. Students complete them orally, first chorally, then individually. E.g., "I always _____ (pack/bring) my _____ (swimsuit/towel)." "Do you like to _____ (swim/build) sandcastles?" "We are _____ (going to/like to) the beach." The teacher listens for accurate grammar and pronunciation of /b/ and /p/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Picture Prompt & Pair Drills:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of beach items. In pairs, students take turns making sentences using 'going to' and 'imperatives'. E.g., (Teacher shows 'cooler') Student A: "I am going to bring a cooler." (Teacher shows 'sunscreen') Student B: "Please pack sunscreen." Students are encouraged to use numbers for quantities (e.g., 'I need two towels.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Pair Interaction – Q&A Chain:</b> Students work in pairs. Student A asks, "What do you always bring to the beach?" Student B answers, "I always bring [item]." Then Student B asks, "Are you going to the beach this weekend?" Student A replies, "Yes, I am going to [action, e.g., swim and relax]." They practice blending sounds like /p/ /æk/ in 'pack' and focusing on clarity. The teacher circulates, providing feedback and correcting pronunciation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Trip to the Beach".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Paired role-play and collaborative discussion – "Plan Our Beach Trip!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Their task is to plan a trip to the beach together, discussing what they will bring and what they are going to do.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each pair receives a simple prompt: "You are planning a trip to the beach with your friend. Talk about what you always pack, what you are going to bring this time, and what you want to do."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students must initiate and sustain the conversation, ask and answer questions about plans, use 'Present Simple' for routines, 'Going to' for future plans, and 'Imperatives' for polite suggestions (e.g., "Please bring the beach ball!"). They should also try to use numbers for quantities (e.g., "I need two towels").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages them to use vocabulary such as 'beach', 'towel', 'sunscreen', 'swimsuit', 'sun hat', 'cooler', 'pack', 'bring', 'swim', 'relax', 'build', 'collect', 'fun', 'sunny', 'always'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students practice their dialogue for 5-7 minutes. The teacher circulates, listening for fluency, interaction, and appropriate use of target language.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each pair presents their "Plan Our Beach Trip!" dialogue to another pair or briefly to the class (1-2 pairs, time permitting). They demonstrate their conversation about packing and activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /b/ and /p/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., swimsuit, towel, sunscreen, beach ball, sun hat).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of Present Simple, 'Going to', and Imperatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to initiate and respond to questions to sustain a conversation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and clear turn-taking with peer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Description of quantities using numbers (e.g., "I need two hats.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I can ask my friend: '___________________________?'"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can say: 'I am going to _________________________ and I always pack _________________________.'"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">6-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me to talk about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word or sentence I learned to say today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., Most students actively participated in pair work; specific grammar points like 'going to' were used effectively.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face? (e.g., Some students struggled to initiate conversations; consistent use of polite imperatives needs more practice; distinguishing /b/ and /p/ in spontaneous speech was difficult for a few.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition)? (e.g., Provide sentence frames for writing to support sentence construction based on their spoken plans; review target vocabulary and phrases related to beach activities; offer more listening practice with similar dialogues.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), students will use the ideas and vocabulary from today's speaking activities to write short sentences or a paragraph about their dream beach trip or what they always pack for a picnic at the beach, reinforcing the theme "I Always Pack Lunch."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">4-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>